  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday and Saturday night: An upper shortwave system exits to\nthe east, while high pressure builds in. Some light snow \nchances will linger near the Central Panhandle and Camas Prairie\nin the morning. Otherwise weather will be dry through Saturday \nnight. Low clouds and fog are expected around the Columbia Basin\ninto the Palouse and Spokane/C'dA area, as well as into the \nsheltered northern valleys. Some of the fog could be locally \ndense in the morning. The REFS shows the best opportunity for \nvisibility near a half-mile or less near Moses Lake to the \nPalouse and Spokane county, gradually eroding from the southeast\nin the morning to afternoon. It is possible, as can often \nhappen, that some areas may hold onto the clouds and fog longer \nespecially over the West Plains to Upper Columbia Basin. \nOtherwise middle to high clouds start increase again Saturday \nnight across the region; but some patchy fog will be possible \nagain Saturday night into Sunday morning, especially over the \nnorth and western basin to northern valleys. Highs will be in \nthe upper 30s to mid-40s. Lows will be in the upper 20s to \nmid-30s.\n\nSunday through Monday: The next frontal system swings in from\nthe Pacific, with more rain and mountain snow. The leading warm\nfront comes in Sunday, while an occluding front pushes in later\nSunday into Sunday night before and opening wave moves in\nMonday. Precipitation chances start to emerge early Sunday\nmorning, rising into the afternoon and become likely by Sunday\nnight into Monday morning. Rain chance remain high through\nMonday, then start to wane from the west Monday evening to \novernight. \n\nSnow levels start near 2000-3000 feet over much of the CWA \nSunday AM, except near 4000-6000 feet over the southern third of\nWA to lower ID as the warm front lifts in. Snow levels rise to \nbetween 2500-6000 feet by the afternoon, lowest near the \nCascades the northern valleys as colder air is held there by the\neast/southeast flow. So look for a chance of a lowland \nrain/snow mix and mountain snow in the afternoon, with that \nrain/snow mix largely shifting toward the Cascade and northern \nmountain valleys, while the rest of the lowlands turn to rain. \nSnow amounts around 2 to 4 inches are possible around Steven and\nSherman Pass, locally higher heading toward Washington Pass \nwest of Mazama. Less than an inch is forecast at Snoqualmie and \nLookout Pass. Rain amounts between 0.25 to 0.50\" are forecast, \nlocally only up to 0.10\" over the deeper basin and up to 0.75\" \nover the Idaho Panhandle valleys. \n\nBreezy conditions are forecast for later Monday with the exiting\nsystem, as winds turn from east to south/southwest. Gusts of\n15-25 mph will be possible over the Upper Columbia Basin, \nPalouse/Spokane/C'dA area, even into north and central Idaho. \nWinds gradually wane into Tuesday. Highs will be in the mid-30s\nto mid-40s Sunday, warming toward the upper 30s and upper 40s\nMonday. Lows will largely be in the 30s.\n\nTuesday to Friday: The Inland NW remains in the progressive\npattern, with periodic waves crossing through the region. The\nmean westerly flow will keep some snow chances alive in the\nmountain zones through the week. A more organized system \nexpands rain chances across lowlands too on Wednesday night into\nThursday. Some snow could mix in to northern valleys too. Highs\nwill be in the upper 30s to mid-40s, with lows in the mid-20s \nto mid-30s. /Solveig",
